.Dd October 17, 2026
.Dt ZPOOL-CLONEDUP 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zpool-clonedup
.Nd deduplicate a pool after the fact with block cloning
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zpool
.Cm clonedup
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl f Ns | Ns Fl q
.Op Fl n
.Fl a Ns | Ns Ar pool Ns ...
.Nm zpool
.Cm clonedup
.Fl p
.Fl a Ns | Ns Ar pool Ns ...
.Nm zpool
.Cm clonedup
.Fl s
.Fl a Ns | Ns Ar pool Ns ...
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Finds blocks with identical content and makes them share one copy through
block cloning, the mechanism behind
.Fn copy_file_range 2
and the
.Sy block_cloning
feature
.Pq see Xr zpool-features 7 .
It keeps no deduplication table, since the Block Reference Table already
counts the references to each shared block.
Between runs the pool keeps a small progress record.
It holds the transaction group in which the last completed default or full
run started, shown as the
.Sy last_clonedup_txg
pool property
.Pq see Xr zpoolprops 7 ,
and the counts of the last run.
.Pp
A run has three phases.
First it walks the pool's block pointers the way a scrub does, without
reading any file data, and indexes in memory the blocks written since the
last completed run.
Then it walks the block pointers again and looks up every older block in
that index, which matches new data against everything already in the pool.
Finally a background apply thread redirects one block of each match onto
the other.
An equal checksum strong enough for dedup is trusted, as dedup trusts it.
Any other match is read and compared byte for byte first.
.Pp
The index may use at most
.Sy zfs_clonedup_scan_mem_max
bytes, or by default the share of memory a scrub may use for its queues
.Pq see Sy zfs_scan_mem_lim_fact No in Xr zfs 4 .
When the new data does not fit, the run splits the checksum space into
partitions and repeats the phases for each one, so the index stays within
the cap.
.Pp
A copy on a device that is being removed, or on the indirect device that a
finished removal leaves, never survives, but the run can redirect it.
ZFS never remaps a cloned block pointer, so a survivor there would keep the
indirect mapping for good.
When every copy of the data is on such a device, the run redirects none of
them.
A copy that is not a gang block survives over gang copies of the same data,
and the run frees the gang copies.
Next, a copy on a special vdev survives.
The run never redirects a copy on a special vdev onto a block elsewhere, so
a block that
.Sy special_small_blocks
put there stays there.
Otherwise the run picks the surviving copy so that space is actually freed.
A block held by a snapshot cannot be freed, so it survives and the run
redirects the other copies onto it.
A run never rewrites a file system or volume that nothing has written to
since its latest snapshot, whether it is mounted or not.
Its blocks serve only as surviving copies.
All its data sits in that snapshot, so a redirect there would write past the
snapshot, add to the used space and free nothing.
A snapshot taken during a run, with nothing written after it, has the same
effect for the rest of the run, which counts the dataset's pairs as policy
skips.
When snapshots hold every copy, the run still redirects the live file's
block in a dataset that has changed since its latest snapshot, so later
snapshots share it.
The space returns when the old snapshots are destroyed.
Until then the redirect adds the block to the used space of the dataset and
its parents, so the run skips it where a quota has no room for the block.
Among copies that are otherwise equal, the run prefers one in a 16 MiB
region of its vdev that already holds a reference table entry, since ZFS
searches the table before it frees any block in such a region.
.Pp
The walks share a scrub's scan code and limits:
.Sy zfs_scrub_min_time_ms
per transaction group and the scrub I/O priority on every device.
The verify reads also use the scrub priority and are not cached, and the
bytes they have in flight stay within the pool-wide limit that a scrub
derives from
.Sy zfs_scan_vdev_limit .
The counters in the kstat
.Pa zfs/ Ns Ar pool Ns Pa /clonedup
start at zero when the pool is imported or created and add up across every
run and dry run.
They include the walks, the blocks read to verify a match, the clones
made, the pairs whose data turned out to differ, the times the apply
thread stepped aside for an administrative operation, and
.Sy mem_peak ,
the most memory the index has held at once.
.Sy walk_resumes
counts the transaction groups that resumed a walk where an earlier one
suspended it.
.Sy canceled_runs
counts the runs and dry runs that ended before they finished.
.Pp
A run never touches:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Snapshots, whose blocks serve as the surviving copy only.
.It
File systems and volumes that nothing has written to since their latest
snapshot, whose blocks also serve as the surviving copy only.
.It
Encrypted datasets.
.It
Datasets with
.Sy readonly Ns = Ns Sy on .
.It
Blocks written with
.Sy dedup Ns = Ns Sy on ,
which the deduplication table already shares.
.It
Blocks whose datasets have
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy off ,
and the files of datasets with
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy source ,
which can only be the surviving copy.
See
.Xr zfsprops 7 .
.It
Datasets in the middle of a
.Nm zfs Cm receive .
.It
Embedded and redacted blocks, and every block that is not file or volume
data.
.It
Blocks written with
.Sy checksum Ns = Ns Sy off
or
.Sy checksum Ns = Ns Sy noparity .
.It
File systems and volumes that nothing has mounted or open and whose intent
log still needs replaying.
.It
Immutable files.
.It
Files whose owner, group or project has reached its block quota.
.El
A run does rewrite append-only files, as
.Fn copy_file_range 2
can clone into them.
.Pp
Two copies of the same data compressed with different algorithms or levels
have different checksums, so a run does not find them.
Data that does not compress is stored uncompressed under any setting, so its
copies still match.
Between two datasets, a run clones only where
.Fn copy_file_range 2
would under
.Sy zfs_bclone_strict_properties
.Pq see Xr zfs 4 ,
except that it ignores
.Sy special_small_blocks ,
because the copy on a special vdev survives.
It pairs only blocks with the same number of copies, whatever
.Sy zfs_bclone_strict_properties
says.
A gang block has as many copies as its gang header.
With
.Sy redundant_metadata Ns = Ns Sy all ,
the default, the gang header of file or volume data has one copy more than
the data, up to three.
There a gang copy pairs with a copy that is not a gang block only when that
copy has one more copy of the data, or when both have three.
The run redirects a block onto a gang block only when the block holds a
single copy of its data, since a gang block pointer does not tell how many
copies its data has.
It counts every other such pair as a policy skip.
It skips clones into a dataset at its quota or refquota, or into a pool that
is out of space.
In mounted filesystems and open volumes, the run rewrites blocks under the
object's range lock.
For a pair it compares byte for byte, it also keeps the source block from
changing until the clone is committed.
The apply thread owns an unmounted filesystem or a closed volume while it
writes to it, the way a mount or a volume open owns a dataset.
Each
.Nm zfs Cm destroy ,
.Nm zfs Cm promote
and
.Nm zfs Cm rollback ,
the end of each
.Nm zfs Cm receive ,
and each
.Nm zfs Cm program
run without
.Fl n
asks the thread to let go of everything it holds, whichever dataset the
operation names.
So on a pool where snapshots are destroyed often, the apply steps aside
often.
An operation that owns a dataset, such as
.Nm zfs Cm mount ,
asks only when it finds that dataset busy, and so do
.Nm zfs Cm send
of a file system and
.Nm zfs Cm release
of the last hold on a snapshot marked for deferred destroy.
Each of these waits at most
.Sy zfs_clonedup_yield_timeout_ms
and then goes ahead.
It fails with EBUSY only where the thread still holds a dataset it needs.
Opening a volume, or resizing one that is not open, asks without waiting and
retries until the thread lets go, and fails with EBUSY if that takes longer.
After letting go, the thread holds nothing until the operation has
completed.
.Pp
Redirecting a block gives it a new logical birth time.
For an incremental
.Nm zfs Cm send ,
redirecting a block written since the last snapshot adds nothing to the
stream, which carries that block anyway.
Redirecting a block that an earlier snapshot already holds makes the stream
carry it again.
To avoid that, run
.Nm zpool Cm clonedup
before taking the snapshot that will be sent, or set
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy source
on replicated datasets.
A redirected block also counts as a change to its dataset since the last
snapshot.
A run rewrites only datasets written to since their latest snapshot, so it
leaves alone a dataset that changes only by
.Nm zfs Cm receive ,
and incremental receives into it still apply without
.Fl F .
.Pp
Only one scan runs at a time, and a run uses the pool's one scan record.
Once a run starts,
.Nm zpool Cm status
no longer shows the result of the last scrub.
It still recommends a scrub after a sequential rebuild exactly when it would
have if the run had left the record alone.
A run posts
.Sy clonedup_start
when it starts,
.Sy clonedup_paused
when it is paused,
.Sy clonedup_resume
when it resumes,
.Sy clonedup_abort
when it is stopped and
.Sy clonedup_finish
when it completes
.Pq see Xr zpool-events 8 .
A dry run posts the same events, and no run posts a scrub or resilver
event.
The restart after an export or a crash posts no event.
A run that a scrub, a resilver or another run replaces posts no event of
its own, as a scrub that a resilver replaces posts none, and the scan that
replaces it posts its own start event.
A scrub or resilver request cancels a running clonedup, and so does
.Nm zpool Cm scrub Fl s ,
but
.Nm zpool Cm scrub Fl p
leaves it running, and
.Nm zpool Cm scrub Fl e
fails while a run is in progress.
The next run repeats the canceled work, since
.Sy last_clonedup_txg
advances only when a run completes.
A completed run advances it past the pairs it skipped too, for example
because a dataset was busy, so only a full run finds those again.
Among clonedup runs, a full run
.Pq Fl f
replaces a running quick
.Pq Fl q
or default run, and a default run replaces a quick one.
A real run of any rank replaces a dry run.
Otherwise a request of equal or lower rank fails with EBUSY, and so does
a request made while a scrub, error scrub, resilver or sequential rebuild
is in progress, a paused scrub included.
.Pp
A run needs the
.Sy clonedup
and
.Sy block_cloning
features enabled, and
.Sy zfs_bclone_enabled
must not be zero.
Setting it to zero stops a running apply before its next group of
blocks, until it is set again.
Of the two features, a dry run needs only
.Sy block_cloning .
The
.Sy clonedup
feature is active only while a run is in progress.
After an export or a crash, a run begins its current partition again, and
the counts it reports from then on cover only the partitions after the
restart.
.Pp
On a pool that only receives streams, every block is held by a snapshot
and no dataset has changed since its latest one, so a run there rewrites
nothing.
.Bl -tag -width "-a"
.It Fl a
Run on all pools.
.It Fl f
Index every block instead of only those written since the last completed
run, and skip the matching walk.
Only a full run finds duplicates among data that existed before the last
run.
.It Fl n
Dry run.
Perform every phase and make the checks a real run makes, but clone nothing,
and report what a real run would have saved.
A dry run writes no scan state, history entry or feature activation of its
own.
A request for a dry run fails while another run is in progress, unless it
resumes a paused dry run with the same options.
The pool history records any pause or stop of the dry run.
It needs a writable pool, reads blocks to verify matches, and holds files and
datasets as a real run does, so it delays the operations described above.
It exists only in memory, so an export or a reboot ends it, and
.Nm zpool Cm status
then reports nothing about it.
It does not advance
.Sy last_clonedup_txg .
.It Fl p
Pause a running clonedup.
To resume it, run
.Nm zpool Cm clonedup
again with the
.Fl f ,
.Fl q
and
.Fl n
flags it was started with.
A request with other flags is ranked against the paused run as described
above.
A paused run stays paused across an export, a reboot or a crash, and
begins its current partition again when it resumes.
This never pauses a scrub.
Cannot be combined with other options except
.Fl a .
Fails when no clonedup run is in progress.
.It Fl q
Quick.
Index the new blocks and clone duplicates among them only, without matching
them against older data.
A quick run does not advance
.Sy last_clonedup_txg ,
so the next run without this flag matches the same blocks against older
data.
.It Fl s
Stop a running clonedup.
This never stops a scrub.
Cannot be combined with other options except
.Fl a .
Fails when no clonedup run is in progress.
.It Fl w
Wait until the run has completed before returning.
.El
.Pp
.Nm zpool Cm status
names a full or quick run, and a dry run, in its scan line, so it gives the
flags that resume a paused run.
It also shows the phase and partition a run is in and how far the current
walk has got.
While the run applies, it shows how much of the apply is done, the blocks
cloned and the space saved, and the part of that space that returns only
when snapshots are destroyed.
A dry run shows the blocks it would clone and the space it would reclaim,
and the number of candidates once it completes.
.Nm zpool Cm status Fl j
reports the same figures under names that begin with
.Sy clonedup_ ,
and breaks
.Sy clonedup_skipped
down by reason.
.Sy clonedup_skipped_stale
counts the candidates whose block changed or was freed after the walk
found it,
.Sy clonedup_skipped_dirty
those whose file held changes not yet written,
.Sy clonedup_skipped_differs
those whose data did not match,
.Sy clonedup_skipped_busy
those the run found busy or left when it stopped, and
.Sy clonedup_skipped_policy
those that a property, a file flag, a quota or another rule kept out.
.Sy clonedup_min_txg
is the
.Sy last_clonedup_txg
the run started from, or 0 for a full run.
The run indexes only blocks born after it.
.Sy clonedup_max_txg
is the transaction group the run started in, which a completed default or
full run makes the new
.Sy last_clonedup_txg .
.Pp
A walk counts each block pointer it visits at its deflated size, the size
.Sy bclonesaved
uses, so a block that block cloning or deduplication shares counts once for
each reference to it.
Each walk starts its count at zero.
A walk over the whole pool shows that count against a total that the run
takes when it starts and again when each later partition starts.
The total is the pool's deflated allocation plus the space block cloning
and deduplication save.
A walk over only the data written since
.Sy last_clonedup_txg
shows no total.
On a pool that nothing writes to during the run, the walk never passes the
total.
A dataset with
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy off
is not walked, but a walk over the whole pool counts its space as walked.
The space saved counts each redirected block once, at its allocated size
rather than its deflated size, at one of its references.
If the run skips that reference, the block counts nothing.
If the run redirects that reference but skips another, the block counts in
full although the other reference keeps it allocated.
The run never rewrites a reference in a dataset with
.Sy clonedup Ns = Ns Sy source
or in one that nothing has written to since its latest snapshot, so a block
that such a reference keeps counts nothing.
.Pp
.Xr zpool-wait 8
can wait for the
.Sy clonedup
activity.
The bytes it reports as remaining are the total less the current walk's
count, which means little during a walk that shows no total or while the
run applies.
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 : No Deduplicating data written since the last run
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm clonedup Fl w Ar tank
.
.Ss Example 2 : No Estimating the space a full run would reclaim
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm clonedup Fl f Fl n Fl w Ar tank
.Dl # Nm zpool Cm status Ar tank
.
